===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*per-}}
From (Northumbrian) {{inh|en|ang|fǣra}}, aphetic form of {{m|ang|ġefēra}} (whence also {{cog|enm|y-fere}}).

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|en|pheer|feer}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|dialectal|or|obsolete}} A [[companion]], [[comrade]] or friend.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1485|author=Sir Thomas Malory|title=Le Morte Darthur|section=Book V
|passage=they swange oute their swerdis and slowe of noble men of armys mo than an hondred – and than they rode ayen to theire '''ferys'''.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A person's [[spouse]], or an animal's [[mate]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=IV|canto=III|stanza=52|passage=And Cambel tooke Cambrina to his '''fere'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1830|author=[[w:Alfred_Tennyson%2C_1st_Baron_Tennyson|Alfred, Lord Tennyson]]|title=[http://www.telelib.com/authors/T/TennysonAlfred/verse/juvenilia/supposedconfessions.html Supposed Confessions of a Second-Rate Sensitive Mind]
|passage=The lamb rejoiceth in the year, / And raceth freely with his '''fere''', / And answers to his mother’s calls / From the flower’d furrow.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1864|author=George MacDonald|title=The Old Nurse's Story
|passage=What if my Duncan be the youth whom his wicked brother hurled into the ravine, come again in a new body, to live out his life on the earth, cut short by his brother’s hatred? If so, his persecution of you, and of your mother for your sake, is easy to understand. And if so, you will never be able to rest till you find your '''fere''', wherever she may have been born on the face of the earth.}}

